{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2712","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2712","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2712\",\"parent_revision\":\"b0977d280e5f656e5c0fe388e5ba070ba3744eb839f1400a94ec042a3080b4de\",\"root_revision\":\"b0977d280e5f656e5c0fe388e5ba070ba3744eb839f1400a94ec042a3080b4de\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-07-08\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`merge_layer.py`'s fragment-merge process removes every graph edge attributed to a re-extracted source file, but nothing in the merge process can re-add them.\\n\\nWhere observed: PR #2626 code review, `plugins/development-harness/scripts/merge_layer.py`.\\n\\nImpact: any source file that owns graph edges loses them the next time it is re-extracted, and this pipeline has no way to bring them back \u2014 edge count for the affected file can only shrink over successive re-extractions, a slow silent data-loss path for the DH workflow knowledge graph.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:36:41.510333Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM 1: merge_layer.py exists at `plugins/development-harness/scripts/merge_layer.py` and performs fragment-merge\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: git show main:plugins/development-harness/scripts/merge_layer.py\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: \\\"fatal: path 'plugins/development-harness/scripts/merge_layer.py' does not exist in 'main'\\\"\\n  - The file does not exist in the main branch or in PR #2626\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The issue description incorrectly names the file. The actual buggy code is in `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` (in PR #2626, not yet merged to main), not a file named `merge_layer.py`. The RT-ICA analysis correctly identified this discrepancy.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 2: The actual file `assemble_graph.py::self_check()` cannot restore deleted edges\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::self_check() (PR #2626, lines 829-875)\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: |\\n      def self_check(nodes: list[dict[str, Any]], edges: list[dict[str, Any]]) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:\\n          \\\"\\\"\\\"Remove orphan edges; warn about overlay types with zero instances...\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n          node_ids = {n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n          clean_edges = []\\n          for edge in edges:\\n              src, tgt = edge[\\\"source\\\"], edge[\\\"target\\\"]\\n              if src not in node_ids or tgt not in node_ids:\\n                  print(f\\\"ORPHAN REMOVED: edge {edge['id']} src={src}(...) tgt={tgt}(...)\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n              else:\\n                  clean_edges.append(edge)\\n          return clean_edges\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::assemble() (PR #2626, lines 946-971)\\n  - Finding: assemble() calls load_layers() which always reads fresh from JSON files, then calls self_check() to remove orphans. The removed edges are discarded into sorted_edges; no re-add mechanism exists anywhere in the workflow. Line 964 tracks `orphans_removed` but the edges themselves are permanently lost.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: self_check() filters edges by checking if source and target node IDs exist. Orphans are removed and never restored. The function has no backup, cache, or mechanism to recover deleted edges \u2014 they are simply excluded from the returned clean_edges list.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 3: Edge count for re-extracted file can only shrink over successive re-extractions\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py (PR #2626)\\n  - Lines 141-180: load_layers() calls _load_json(path) which reads fresh from disk every time. No prior edge data is loaded or merged.\\n  - Lines 40-46: _node_id() builds node IDs via `f\\\"{prefix}.{slugify(key)}\\\"`. Changes to source file content (agent name, artifact id, fork_id) produce new node IDs.\\n  - Lines 829-875: self_check() removes edges whose source/target IDs are absent from the current node set.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Scenario: Source file re-extracted with changed artifact ID. Old edges reference old node ID (e.g., \\\"artifact.old_id\\\"). New extraction produces new node ID (e.g., \\\"artifact.new_id\\\"). \\n  - Result: load_layers() starts fresh with only new nodes. self_check() finds old edges orphaned and removes them permanently. Next extraction: same old edges remain deleted, edge count stays lower or shrinks further if more fields change.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Because load_layers() always starts fresh from JSON files (no prior state is carried forward), and self_check() irreversibly removes orphan edges, edge count can only stay the same or decrease across re-extractions. It cannot increase \u2014 deleted edges are never restored.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 4: This is a slow silent data-loss path for the DH workflow knowledge graph\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::self_check() (PR #2626, line 849)\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: |\\n      print(f\\\"ORPHAN REMOVED: edge {edge['id']} src={src}(...) tgt={tgt}(...)\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Data visibility: self_check() prints to stderr only (line 849). The final dh-workflow-graph.json output (line 968) contains no record of which edges were deleted, why, or when.\\n  - Persistence: Orphan removals are logged to stderr in real-time but not stored in any durable artifact (no removal log, no deletion history).\\n  - From consumer perspective: Any tool reading dh-workflow-graph.json sees edge count decrease across versions with no explanation in the JSON itself.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: While self_check() does print \\\"ORPHAN REMOVED\\\" messages to stderr, these are transient console output. The permanent data loss (missing edges in the output JSON) is invisible to downstream consumers of the graph. An observer running the tool may see stderr output; but the graph output file itself provides no audit trail, making it appear as if edges \\\"silently\\\" vanish between versions. This qualifies as \\\"slow silent data loss\\\" from the perspective of the graph consumer.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py (PR #2626, not merged to main)\\n  FILE_PATHS_VERIFIED: \\n    - merge_layer.py: does NOT exist (git show main:...merge_layer.py returns \\\"fatal: path ... does not exist\\\")\\n    - assemble_graph.py: PR #2626 [verified via git show pr-2626:plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py]\\n  VERIFIED_BY: Bash git commands + source code inspection on 2026-07-06\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:43:52.941464Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: fix: merge_layer.py permanently deletes graph edges on re-extraction with no restoration path\\nDate: 2026-07-06\\nGoal: Prevent permanent silent loss of graph edges when assemble_graph.py re-runs after source data changes.\\nConditions:\\n1. assemble_graph.py exists in PR #2626 branch (pr-2626) at plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. self_check() iterates edges and removes orphans by checking source/target node IDs against current nodes set | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. load_layers() rebuilds fresh from JSON files every run \u2014 no prior state preserved | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. _node_id(prefix, key) generates IDs as f\\\"{prefix}.{slugify(key)}\\\" \u2014 deterministic from source key | Status: AVAILABLE\\n5. Node IDs ARE expected to change when source data changes (fork renamed, skill name changes, artifact key changes) | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 DERIVED from code: IDs are deterministic slugs, so any source key change produces a new ID; old edges referencing old IDs become orphans\\n6. The ID stability contract (derived): IDs should be stable for unchanged inputs but will change when source data changes \u2014 the problem is deletion with no restoration path, not ID instability itself | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 DERIVED from _node_id implementation and load_layers always-fresh rebuild pattern\\n7. No test coverage exists for self_check() | Status: AVAILABLE\\n8. dh-workflow-explorer.html consumes dh-workflow-graph.json output silently | Status: AVAILABLE\\n9. 14 upstream JSON layer files can trigger orphan creation on any key change | Status: AVAILABLE\\n10. Intended behavior for orphan edges is to preserve them with status annotation, not delete | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 inferred from: (a) edges encode relationships built by agents with significant effort, (b) the graph-schema.md documents edge types as persistent structural knowledge, (c) no documentation anywhere mandates edge deletion on re-extraction\\n11. Workflow-extractor ID stability contract | Status: AVAILABLE \u2014 DERIVED: code shows IDs are deterministic slugs from source keys; ID changes are expected when source changes; fix approach is \\\"preserve orphaned edges with status:orphan annotation\\\" NOT \\\"prevent ID changes\\\" \u2014 this is derivable from the _node_id function signature and load_layers rebuild behavior\\nAVAILABLE count: 9\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\nOverall verdict: READY\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n> **Scope note:** `assemble_graph.py` does NOT exist on the `main` branch. It lives exclusively on PR branch `claude/workflow-extractor-ensemble` (PR #2626). All impact below is conditional on that PR merging. Current blast radius on `main` is **zero**. The analysis addresses post-merge state.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::self_check()` \u2014 PR #2626 branch only. Receives the fully assembled in-memory node list and edge list. Filters out any edge whose `source` or `target` ID is absent from the current node set. Returns the cleaned edge list with no audit trail beyond a `print()` to stderr. The return value becomes the `edges` array written to `dh-workflow-graph.json`. There is no diff, no dry-run mode, no backup of removed edges. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Destructive mutation of the assembled graph with no restoration path. If stderr is not captured, removed edges leave no record.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::assemble()` \u2014 PR #2626 branch only. Orchestrator for the full pipeline: calls `_build_all_nodes()`, `_build_all_edges()`, then `self_check()`, then writes to the output path. The `self_check()` call is the last transformation before disk write \u2014 its output is the final record. | Risk: HIGH | Why: No mechanism to detect edge loss between pre-self_check and post-self_check edge counts. Silently narrows the graph on every run when IDs drift.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L0-forks.json` \u2014 Source of decision-fork node IDs. `assemble_graph.py::build_decision_nodes()` derives node IDs from fields in this file (`fork_id`). Re-extraction by `workflow-extractor` can silently change these IDs, orphaning edges that referenced the old values. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: ID instability in this file is the upstream trigger for the orphan-deletion bug. No ID stability contract is enforced between extraction passes.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-work-backlog-item.json`, `L1-trace-complete-implementation.json`, `L1-trace-work-milestone.json`, `L1-trace-feature-skills.json` \u2014 Source of trace edges and step node IDs used by `build_branch_edges_from_l1()`. Re-extraction renames these IDs. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Same ID drift trigger as L0. Four separate files covering different workflow routes; any one of them can orphan cross-file edges.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G2-artifacts.json`, `G4-concurrency.json`, `G8-backend-routing.json` \u2014 Sources for artifact, agent, MCP tool node IDs (`build_artifact_nodes()`, `build_agent_nodes()`, `build_mcp_tool_nodes()`). Re-extraction changes agent names or artifact ids. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Cross-layer edges (e.g., step \u2192 agent \u2192 mcp_tool) span multiple layer files; ID drift in any source file orphans edges connecting to it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` \u2014 The assembled output written by `assemble()`. This file is permanently modified each time `assemble_graph.py` runs. Orphaned edges silently disappear from this file on each run after re-extraction. Since this file is committed to the repo, the git history could theoretically recover old versions, but there is no programmatic restoration path in the tool itself. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The assembled graph is the source of truth for the explorer. Silent edge loss corrupts the displayed workflow topology with no in-band indicator that connections are missing.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` \u2014 Reads graph data via an inlined `GRAPH_DATA` constant embedded in the HTML at build time (comment: \\\"no fetch needed for file:// protocol\\\"). The Cytoscape.js rendering renders only the nodes and edges present in `GRAPH_DATA`. Orphaned edges do not appear as errors \u2014 the graph simply renders with fewer connections. The overlay filtering system (workflow, agents, skills, instructions, tools, artifacts) silently omits the missing edges. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The explorer has no completeness indicator. A user analyzing the DH workflow topology sees a silently incomplete graph with no visual indication of missing connections.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G1-failure-modes.json`, `G3-actor-types.json`, `G5-optionality.json`, `G6-task-types.json`, `G7-complexity-routing.json` \u2014 Annotation layer files. Used by `assemble_graph.py` node builders to enrich nodes with metadata. Their node type/identifier fields anchor edge source/target IDs for annotation-layer edges. Re-extraction can change these identifiers. | Risk: LOW | Why: These layers are primarily node metadata rather than edge source/target anchors; risk is lower than L0/L1/G2/G4/G8 but still contributes to the overall ID-drift surface.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-trace-methodology.md` \u2014 Describes the multi-phase collection protocol (A/B/C/D) that produces the layer JSON files. Does not specify node ID stability requirements across re-extraction passes. | Risk: LOW | Why: Not a direct code dependency, but its absence of ID-stability guidance enables the conditions that trigger the bug.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- None identified. No test file anywhere in the repository covers `assemble_graph.py`, `self_check()`, edge integrity, or the assembled graph output. The `plugins/development-harness/tests/` directory contains tests for backlog operations, dispatch state, reconciliation, and artifact registry \u2014 not graph assembly.\\n\\n  Risk: HIGH | Why: Without a test that asserts edge count before and after `self_check()`, or that detects when a re-extraction run produces fewer edges than the prior run, the bug is invisible until a developer manually inspects the explorer output.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` \u2014 Documents the schema of `dh-workflow-graph.json` and the edge types. States that `assemble_graph.py` produces the file and `dh-workflow-explorer.html` consumes it. Does not document the destructive behavior of `self_check()`, the conditions that trigger orphan removal, or the absence of a restoration path. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The schema document is what future developers consult when building or modifying the assembler. Its silence on the destructive edge-removal behavior means the hazard is invisible to contributors.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/COVERAGE.md` \u2014 Notes \\\"Graph/explorer rebuild from L0+L1+G1-G8\\\" as PENDING. Does not document node ID stability requirements between extraction passes, nor the risk that re-extraction silently orphans cross-layer edges. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The coverage manifest is the entry point for anyone running a follow-up extraction pass. Without a warning about ID stability, a re-extraction pass that renames IDs will silently corrupt the assembled graph on the next rebuild.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- None identified. No CI workflow in `.github/workflows/` references `assemble_graph.py`, `dh-workflow-graph.json`, or any graph assembly step. The script has no automated execution path; it is run manually. This means no CI pipeline catches edge count regression between assembly runs.\\n\\n  Note: the absence of CI coverage is itself a risk factor \u2014 if the script were added to CI, the destructive behavior would occur automatically on every re-extraction, with no gate to detect graph degradation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` \u2014 The agent that produces the layer JSON files (L0, L1, G1-G8) that `assemble_graph.py` reads. Its extraction rules (\\\"VERBATIM. Quote labels exactly\\\") do not specify an ID stability contract. Node IDs in the layer files are derived from label text via `slugify()` in the assembler \u2014 if the extractor changes a label (corrects a typo, rewrites a heading, normalises whitespace), the slugified ID changes and all edges referencing that ID become orphans. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The agent actively produces the inputs to the buggy assembler. Its extraction rules are silent on ID stability. Any improvement pass by the extractor can silently trigger orphan-edge deletion on the next assembly run.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/.claude/rules/workflow-extraction.md` \u2014 Specifies that `dh:workflow-extractor` must be used for all extraction agents. Defines the schema-after-data-collection discipline. Does not mention node ID stability, the orphan-deletion risk in `self_check()`, or any pre/post-assembly edge count verification step. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: This rule file is the behavioral contract read by orchestrators dispatching extraction agents. Its silence on ID stability perpetuates the conditions that trigger the bug.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` | Role: producer | Connection: owns `self_check()` \u2014 the function that permanently removes orphan edges | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` | Role: consumer | Connection: assembled output; edges are permanently deleted from this file on each run | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` | Role: consumer | Connection: renders `GRAPH_DATA` (inlined from `dh-workflow-graph.json`); silently omits missing edges | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L0-forks.json` | Role: producer | Connection: source of fork node IDs; re-extraction changes IDs and triggers orphan deletion | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: 'fork_id'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-work-backlog-item.json` | Role: producer | Connection: trace node IDs; re-extraction changes IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-complete-implementation.json` | Role: producer | Connection: trace node IDs; re-extraction changes IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-work-milestone.json` | Role: producer | Connection: trace node IDs; re-extraction changes IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-feature-skills.json` | Role: producer | Connection: trace node IDs; re-extraction changes IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G2-artifacts.json` | Role: producer | Connection: artifact node IDs; re-extraction renames artifact ids | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G4-concurrency.json` | Role: producer | Connection: dispatch topology node IDs; re-extraction renames agent IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G8-backend-routing.json` | Role: producer | Connection: MCP tool node IDs; re-extraction renames tool IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G1-failure-modes.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer anchored to node IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G3-actor-types.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer anchored to node IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G5-optionality.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer anchored to node IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G6-task-types.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer anchored to node IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer anchored to node IDs | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: documents edge schema and assembly pipeline; silent on destructive self_check() behavior | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/COVERAGE.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: entry point for re-extraction passes; silent on ID stability and orphan-edge risk | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-trace-methodology.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: defines collection protocol; no ID stability contract | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: produces layer JSON files; re-extraction changes node IDs | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/.claude/rules/workflow-extraction.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: behavioral contract for extraction passes; silent on ID stability | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: **HIGH**\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::self_check()` \u2014 destructive permanent edge deletion with no dry-run, no audit trail, and no restoration path built into the tool\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` \u2014 assembled output with no edge-count integrity check; silently loses graph connections on every re-extraction run\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` \u2014 renders the corrupt output without any completeness indicator; users cannot detect missing connections\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - **behavioral** \u2014 `self_check()` logic is correct as a validator but catastrophic as a silent mutator with no rollback\\n  - **verification** \u2014 zero test coverage for graph assembly, edge integrity, or orphan-edge detection\\n  - **documentation** \u2014 no documentation of the destructive behavior, the ID stability contract, or the restoration gap\\n  - **scope** \u2014 the bug is latent and triggered only at assembly time; every re-extraction pass without ID stability guarantees is a deferred data-loss event\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The 14 layer JSON files are all in scope as upstream triggers. Their ID fields are the upstream input that determines which edges survive. This was not called out in the original item description (which focused on `merge_layer.py` / `self_check()` alone).\\n  - The `workflow-extractor` agent is in scope because its re-extraction passes are the mechanism that changes node IDs. This is an agent-instruction risk, not just a code risk.\\n  - **PR #2626 not merged:** `assemble_graph.py` does not yet exist in `main`. All impact is conditional on merge. The risk assessment above applies post-merge.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible (`assemble_graph.py::self_check()` fixed with dry-run/audit mode or non-destructive edge quarantine; `assemble()` updated to log removed edge count to output JSON)\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible (`dh-workflow-graph.json` regenerated from a clean re-assembly run after fix; `dh-workflow-explorer.html` GRAPH_DATA rebuilt with verified-complete edge set)\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added (unit test for `self_check()` asserting edge count pre/post; integration test asserting assembled edge count matches layer-file edge count)\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated (`graph-schema.md` documents the orphan-removal behavior and its conditions; `COVERAGE.md` adds ID stability warning; `workflow-trace-methodology.md` documents node ID stability contract)\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated (consider adding a graph-assembly step to CI that fails on edge count regression)\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated (`workflow-extractor.md` adds ID stability guidance; `.claude/rules/workflow-extraction.md` documents that re-extraction may trigger orphan deletion)\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved (if the fix is to quarantine rather than delete orphans, document the quarantine schema in `graph-schema.md`)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked (verify that `dh-workflow-graph.json` is not consumed by any system beyond the explorer HTML \u2014 grep confirms only `graph-schema.md` references it in main; `dh-system-model.html` may also embed graph data \u2014 verify separately)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **merge_layer.py permanently deletes graph edges on re-extraction with no restoration path** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task - auto-derived from /code-review max --fix pr session, PR #2626\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-07-03\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-07-06)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:45:17.912694Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Check out the `pr-2626` branch.\\n2. Run `assemble_graph.py` to generate the initial `dh-workflow-graph.json`. Record the edge count in the output JSON.\\n3. Change a key in any of the 14 upstream layer JSON files \u2014 for example, rename a `fork_id` in `L0-forks.json`, or rename a skill name in a layer file. This causes `_node_id()` to generate a new node ID for the renamed key, making all edges that referenced the old ID appear as orphans.\\n4. Re-run `assemble_graph.py`.\\n5. Observe that `self_check()` prints `ORPHAN REMOVED` to stderr for each affected edge and permanently removes them from `dh-workflow-graph.json`. The edge count in the output JSON is now lower than in step 2.\\n6. Load `dh-workflow-explorer.html` and observe a silently incomplete graph with no indication that edges are missing.\\n\\nThere is no way to restore the deleted edges without re-introducing the original source keys and re-running extraction.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:45:41.296100Z</sub>\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 P1\\n\\nThe bug causes permanent, silent data loss in a graph that serves as the structural record of the development harness workflow. Evidence from the impact analysis:\\n\\n- Data loss is irreversible within a single run: once `self_check()` removes edges, there is no restoration path in the code.\\n- The deletion is silent to consumers: `dh-workflow-explorer.html` displays a reduced graph with no indication that edges are missing, so analysts and agents cannot detect the corruption without comparing edge counts across runs.\\n- The trigger surface is wide: any key change in any of the 14 upstream layer JSON files (L0-forks.json, G2-artifacts.json, G4-concurrency.json, G8-backend-routing.json, L1-trace-* files, and others) triggers the deletion path.\\n- There is zero test coverage for `self_check()` behavior, meaning regressions cannot be detected automatically.\\n- The affected code lives on the `pr-2626` branch, bounding blast radius to that branch for now, but the deletion logic will enter the main codebase when the branch is merged.\\n\\nPriority is P1 (not P0) because the affected code is on an unmerged branch, limiting the current production impact. It must be resolved before `pr-2626` is merged.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:46:08.028032Z</sub>\\n\\n**Primary defect site:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py::self_check()` on branch `pr-2626` \u2014 permanently removes edges whose source or target node ID is absent from the current nodes set, with no restoration mechanism.\\n\\n**Cascading data loss:**\\n- `dh-workflow-graph.json` (output artifact) \u2014 loses edges silently on every re-extraction after any source key change. The JSON file itself becomes the corrupted record.\\n\\n**Silent consumer exposure:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` \u2014 reads `dh-workflow-graph.json` and displays a reduced graph with no missing-data indicator. Analysts and agents looking at workflow topology receive an incorrect structural picture.\\n\\n**Wide trigger surface:**\\n- 14 upstream layer JSON input files (L0-forks.json, G2-artifacts.json, G4-concurrency.json, G8-backend-routing.json, 4 \u00d7 L1-trace-*.json files, and others) \u2014 a key rename in any one triggers the deletion path for all edges connected to the renamed node.\\n\\n**Upstream extraction agent:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` \u2014 instructs agents to extract layer data to JSON files whose key changes are the proximate trigger for edge deletion.\\n\\n**Documentation gap:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` \u2014 documents edge types as persistent structural knowledge with no mention that edges can be silently deleted on re-extraction.\\n\\n**Zero automated detection:**\\n- No tests exercise `self_check()` behavior; corrupted output cannot be detected by CI.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:46:38.092960Z</sub>\\n\\n- Re-extraction becomes safe to run after source key changes: analysts and agents can update layer JSON files and re-run `assemble_graph.py` without losing structural graph data.\\n- `dh-workflow-graph.json` retains a complete edge record across re-runs, making the graph a reliable source of truth for workflow topology.\\n- `dh-workflow-explorer.html` displays accurate workflow structure; gaps are surfaced as visible indicators rather than silent omissions.\\n- Orphan edges become diagnosable: when a source key changes, the affected edges are identifiable and actionable rather than permanently gone.\\n- Test coverage added for `self_check()` behavior provides a regression gate that catches future changes to edge handling before they reach the main branch.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:47:03.561622Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen `assemble_graph.py` encounters an edge whose source or target node ID is absent from the current nodes set:\\n\\n- The edge is retained in the output rather than deleted. It may be annotated (e.g., with a status field) to indicate that it references a node not present in the current extraction run.\\n- The condition is reported in a way the caller can observe and act on \u2014 not silently discarded to stderr. At minimum, the output must allow the caller to distinguish \\\"extraction succeeded with all edges intact\\\" from \\\"extraction succeeded but some edges reference absent nodes.\\\"\\n- `dh-workflow-graph.json` does not have a lower edge count after a re-run caused solely by a source key rename. Structural data already captured is preserved.\\n- `dh-workflow-explorer.html` is able to render preserved orphan edges or display a visible indicator when edges reference absent nodes, rather than displaying a silently reduced graph.\\n- Running `assemble_graph.py` twice with identical input produces identical output \u2014 the operation is idempotent on edge count.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:47:36.194762Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Run `assemble_graph.py` twice on branch `pr-2626` with no changes to any upstream layer JSON file. The edge count in `dh-workflow-graph.json` is identical between the two runs.\\n\\n2. Rename a key in any one upstream layer JSON file (e.g., rename a `fork_id` in `L0-forks.json`), then re-run `assemble_graph.py`. The edges that previously referenced the old node ID are present in `dh-workflow-graph.json` after the re-run \u2014 they are not deleted from the output.\\n\\n3. After the key rename and re-run in criterion 2, the number of edges in `dh-workflow-graph.json` that are unrelated to the renamed key is unchanged from the pre-rename run.\\n\\n4. The condition described in criterion 2 (edges referencing an absent node) is observable by the caller through a non-stderr mechanism \u2014 for example, a non-zero exit code, a structured field in the JSON output, or an explicit log entry that is not mixed into stderr noise. A caller that does not read stderr must still be able to detect that orphan edges were encountered.\\n\\n5. The reproduction described in the Reproducibility section \u2014 check out `pr-2626`, run `assemble_graph.py`, rename a source key, re-run \u2014 no longer results in an edge count decrease in `dh-workflow-graph.json`.\\n\\n6. A test exists that exercises `self_check()` on an input containing an edge whose node ID is absent from the nodes set, and the test asserts that the edge is preserved (or annotated) rather than deleted.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:48:20.319992Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Code (primary defect) | `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` (branch: pr-2626) \u2014 `self_check()`, `load_layers()`, `_node_id()` |\\n| Code (output artifact) | `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` (branch: pr-2626) |\\n| Code (silent consumer) | `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` (branch: pr-2626) |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` (branch: pr-2626) \u2014 edge type definitions; no deletion semantics documented |\\n| Agent | `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` (branch: pr-2626) \u2014 upstream extraction instructions |\\n| Branch | `pr-2626` \u2014 all affected code lives on this branch; fix must be scoped here before merge |\\n| Fact-check | Issue classifier confirmed: `merge_layer.py` does not exist; defect is in `assemble_graph.py` |\\n| Issue classification | Root cause: `load_layers()` always rebuilds fresh from JSON without preserving prior edge state; any source key change generates new node IDs via `_node_id()`, making prior edges appear as orphans |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:48:00.280027Z</sub>\\n\\nAll files below are on the `pr-2626` branch unless otherwise noted.\\n\\n**Primary defect location:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` \u2014 contains `self_check()` (permanent edge deletion), `load_layers()` (always-fresh rebuild with no prior state), and `_node_id()` (deterministic slug generation; source key changes produce new IDs)\\n\\n**Output artifact (data loss target):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` \u2014 graph output that loses edges silently on re-extraction\\n\\n**Silent consumer:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` \u2014 reads `dh-workflow-graph.json`; displays incomplete graph with no missing-data indicator\\n\\n**Documentation gap:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` \u2014 documents edge types as persistent; no mention that edges can be silently deleted\\n\\n**Upstream extraction agent (trigger path):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` \u2014 instructs agents to produce layer JSON files whose key changes trigger the bug\\n\\n**Upstream layer JSON input files (wide trigger surface \u2014 any key change in any of these triggers edge deletion):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/L0-forks.json`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/G2-artifacts.json`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/G4-concurrency.json`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/G8-backend-routing.json`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/L1-trace-*.json` (4 files)\\n- Additional layer JSON files (impact analysis identified 14 total upstream inputs)\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:48:42.701564Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: PR #2626 being the active working branch \u2014 all affected files (`assemble_graph.py`, `dh-workflow-graph.json`, `dh-workflow-explorer.html`, `graph-schema.md`, upstream layer JSON files) exist only on that branch. The fix must be applied there before the branch is merged to avoid introducing the data-loss behavior into the main codebase.\\n- Blocks: Merging PR #2626 \u2014 the branch should not be merged until this defect is resolved, as merging would introduce permanent edge deletion behavior into the main graph assembly pipeline.\\n- Blocks: None beyond PR #2626 merge gate \u2014 this is a self-contained fix within the graph assembly pipeline on that branch.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:49:15.009021Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium\\n\\nThe defect is isolated to a single file (`assemble_graph.py`) on a single branch (`pr-2626`). The root cause is confirmed and well-understood: `self_check()` deletes edges rather than preserving or annotating them. The behavioral change required is clear from the acceptance criteria.\\n\\nEffort drivers pushing past Small:\\n\\n- The fix must not break the valid purpose of `self_check()` \u2014 detecting genuinely corrupt graph state vs. expected orphan conditions from key changes requires a distinction that may need design input.\\n- `load_layers()` always rebuilds from scratch, so any preservation approach must work within that stateless rebuild pattern.\\n- Test coverage must be added for `self_check()` behavior, which has zero existing tests \u2014 writing representative fixtures for the 14-input layer structure is non-trivial.\\n- `graph-schema.md` requires updating to document the corrected edge-handling semantics.\\n- Validation must confirm that `dh-workflow-explorer.html` correctly renders the preserved or annotated orphan edges without further changes.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:37:17.981936Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: Observable failure with traceable cause chain. The fragment-merge process removes every graph edge attributed to a re-extracted source file through a deterministic mechanism (self_check() filters edges whose node IDs are absent), and nothing in the merge pipeline can restore them. This is a broken implementation path, not a missing validation gate \u2014 the code does exactly what it was written to do, but the implementation is destructive.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys via structured evidence chain from RT-ICA + code mechanism documentation\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Re-extracted source files gradually lose all associated graph edges over successive extractions (silent data loss) \u2192 edges are preserved or restored when node IDs change during re-extraction (data integrity maintained)\\n</div>\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:37:36.014482Z</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via structured evidence from RT-ICA and code mechanism documentation\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n1. **Observed failure**: Source files lose all graph edges when re-extracted with changed node IDs\\n2. **Why**: `self_check()` in `assemble_graph.py` iterates all edges and filters out any whose source or target node ID is absent from the current node set\\n3. **Why**: Re-extraction rebuilds node ID set from scratch via `_node_id(prefix, slugify(key))` based on current fork_id, artifact id, and agent names \u2014 any change produces different IDs than previous extraction\\n4. **Why**: Old node IDs are now absent from the fresh node set, so edges referencing them are categorized as orphans and deleted by self_check()\\n5. **Why** (root cause): `load_layers()` always rebuilds the node set fresh from JSON files without preserving edges from prior extractions \u2014 there is no restoration path in the merge pipeline to re-attach edges after self_check() filters them out\\n\\n**Root cause**: The merge pipeline lacks a restoration mechanism for edges whose node IDs become orphans during re-extraction. `self_check()` permanently deletes orphan edges, and no subsequent step in `load_layers()` can recover them because the graph is rebuilt fresh from JSON each time.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: `assemble_graph.py::self_check()` deletion logic + `merge_layer.py` or `load_layers()` restoration path (missing)\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"c23d67baa45a9bb2e9bdfa82be30bdf8801817cea81858cd63ff67e75339830f\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCJX0w\"}"}